  "method": "Batch of 799 witnesses in 24 problems (`MANIFEST.json` + `records/<record_id>.json` + `SUMMARY.tsv`), each at least 1e-4 (relative) below the current `records/SUMMARY.csv` entry (main at 176e2c2e, checked 2026-10-29). Every record passes `verify_general.py` (VALID) and `certify_general.py` (CERTIFIED) from that commit locally, run on the files extracted from the archive. The changes against the current records: median -1.66 %, largest -13.39 %; 498 records improve by \u2265 1 %, 692 by \u2265 0.1 %.\n\n**Relation to earlier submissions.** #98\u2013#103 (including #101 and #102 from sinkomr) were merged into 176e2c2e on 2026-10-28; every record here is at least 1e-4 (relative) below the value in that archive.\n\n**Against PolyPack.** I also compared with PolyPack's published records (https://github.com/sinkomr/PolyPack, `data/polyhedra/records.json`, fetched 2026-10-29) and kept only records that are at least 1e-4 below both the archive and PolyPack (463 of these records have a PolyPack value; `SUMMARY.tsv` lists both values, `polypack_s`, `change_vs_best`).\n\n**How the records were made.** `found_by`, `credit` and `method` identify where each record starts; `method` or `refinement` gives the steps.\n\n- *Refinements of catalogue records* (238): a contact-preserving descent (sequential linear programming with the contacts as inequalities) started from the catalogue record named in `refinement`. `found_by`, `credit` and `method` are the catalogue record's own, copied verbatim; my part is in `refined_by` and `refinement_credit`. For 199 of them the start was the record for a different n and the first n pieces were kept (`derived_from`). Rigidity analysis and code: https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23630728\n- *Lattice cuts* (476): all pieces in one orientation at the points of a lattice inside the container, orientation and position of the container optimised, then a descent as above.\n- *Cell compositions* (85): the container is divided into cells and each cell is filled with a catalogue record",
